  "url" : "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50108717",
  "answers" : [ "Quick update here since a lot of folks asked about what slopcodebench is &#x2F; where it fits in<p>Slop code bench is unique among benchmarks in that it makes a model work against a progressively disclosed list of features (checkpoints) that it doesn’t know it advance, inheriting the same codebase through the lifecycle of a single multi-checkpoint challenge<p>I like this because it much more mirrors how real software dev happens, rather than most benchmarks with one off tasks where slop code doesn’t hinder future progress<p>The original post[1] and the SOL&#x2F;fable follow up[2] went into a bit more detail on the benchmark and its challenges<p>1. <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=49076391\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=49076391</a><p>2. <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;humanlayer&#x2F;advanced-context-engineering-for-coding-agents&#x2F;blob&#x2F;main&#x2F;benchmarking-sol-fable-kimi-on-slop-code-bench.md\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;humanlayer&#x2F;advanced-context-engineering-f...</a>" ]
